<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austrasia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">561–<a href="/years/843">843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Austrasia was the northeastern realm of the Frankish state in the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, centred on the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and Main rivers and reaching beyond the old Roman frontier on the Rhine. It was one of the subdivisions into which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a> divided their realm, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a>, Aquitaine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and by 561 it was ruled as a separate kingdom by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-i">Sigebert I</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a> had its original power base there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> of 843 divided it between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>, and its western core later came to be called Lotharingia and its eastern parts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…family inheritance, dividing it among their sons, so that it was repeatedly split into kingdoms that came to be known as <b>Austrasia</b> in the east, Neustria in the west and Burgundy, and reunited when lines died out, as under Chlothar I in 558 and Chlothar II in 613.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Dagobert I, king of <b>Austrasia</b> since 623, became ruler of Neustria and Burgundy as well, and so of all the Frankish kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-18">18 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
